---

<DocShell
   title="Screen reader automation that starts in the CLI"
   summary="a11lied is built for the awkward middle ground most accessibility tooling skips: not just static rules, not just raw screen reader driving, but a workflow that can explain the criterion, check whether it applies, gather evidence, and stay honest about what is still manual."
   links={links}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Start here</h2>
      <p>
         If you are testing a page or component, resolve the WCAG criterion first. Then decide whether
         you need lookup, applicability, a low-level driver session, a named pattern, or a verification
         report. Those are different layers on purpose.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What lives where</h2>
      <ul>
         <li><a href="/wcag-data-sources">WCAG data sources</a> explains where standards knowledge comes from.</li>
         <li><a href="/criterion-lookup">Criterion lookup</a> covers search, lookup, and level queries.</li>
         <li><a href="/applicability">Applicability</a> explains why a criterion matters for a target.</li>
         <li><a href="/driver-usage">Driver usage</a> covers the low-level accessibility driver.</li>
         <li><a href="/pattern-execution">Pattern execution</a> covers reusable Guidepup-backed probes.</li>
         <li><a href="/verification-semantics">Verification semantics</a> explains what the verdicts really mean.</li>
         <li><a href="/release-checklist">Release checklist</a> keeps public release claims grounded.</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section class="callout">
      <h2>The blunt version</h2>
      <p>
         a11lied is allowed to collect strong evidence. It is not allowed to fake certainty. That rule
         shows up in the CLI, the MCP server, the Storybook path, the docs, and the agent skill because
         it has to.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
